WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.386 --> 00:00:04.330
CRAIG BOX: Anthos is Google
Cloud's new open platform

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.980
that lets you run an
app anywhere, simply,

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.780
flexibly, and securely.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.150
With Anthos, we bring
the cloud to you.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.880
As an enterprise, you
need to move faster.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.280
The expected time to market
for products and services

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.303
has shortened dramatically
from years to days.

00:00:19.303 --> 00:00:20.720
With the growth
of mobile clients,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.762
you are now serving hundreds
to billions of users

00:00:22.762 --> 00:00:25.970
concurrently, rather than
making copies on disks.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:29.770
But today, you move at the
speed of your slowest develop.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.785
Cloud is a way to
put the pedal down.

00:00:31.785 --> 00:00:33.160
The cloud is
wonderful for people

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.820
who are building
everything from scratch.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.487
But you might have
decades of investment

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:38.200
in your existing data centers.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.780
You are forced into making
trade-offs-- security

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.920
versus agility, reliability
versus cost, portability

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.860
versus consistency.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.410
The news is you don't have
to move to the cloud today.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.330
Moving to a microservices
architecture,

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.660
you can let each team work
at the speed that suits them.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.260
Believe it or not, Google
started out this way, too,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.580
and we have a lot of
investment in data centers.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.330
Changing the way we
wrote our applications

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.970
meant we could move quicker
and scale out to more locations

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.160
to serve our global
customer base.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.037
Along the way, we pioneered
a thing called a container.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:13.620
Containers are a way of bundling
an application for deployment

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.030
on a server.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.040
If you write software
in something like Java,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.630
you might do this today.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.750
Instead of using an application
server like Tomcat or WebSphere

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.270
and bundling your
application into a JAR file,

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.190
you build your application
into a container

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.400
and run it on a
container runtime.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.110
Normally, an application
server handles

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.290
clustering, load balancing, and
resource management for you.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.560
But that's only for
applications on the JVM.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.420
Containers, on the
other hand, run directly

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.520
on the operating
system kernel, meaning

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.685
you can put any
application in them.

00:01:43.685 --> 00:01:45.560
You don't need to have
a different deployment

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.650
system for apps written
in different languages

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.090
or binaries versus things
you've built from source.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.880
Now you just need an application
server to run these containers,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.352
and that's where
Kubernetes comes in.

00:01:56.352 --> 00:01:57.810
Kubernetes deals
with all the stuff

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.900
that your application
server worried about,

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.280
and it does so in
a declarative way.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.780
You tell it what
you want to run,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.907
and it makes sure that you
always have what you asked for.

00:02:06.907 --> 00:02:08.699
You could think of
Kubernetes as the kernel

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:12.730
of the new distributed
application platform.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.940
As with all software, you need
to install, manage, and operate

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.818
your Kubernetes environment.

00:02:17.818 --> 00:02:20.110
You need to manage the nodes
through things like kernel

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.312
and operating system upgrades.

00:02:22.312 --> 00:02:23.770
The manual management
of Kubernetes

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.730
can be resource-intensive.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:30.280
So in 2015, we launched Google
Kubernetes Engine in the cloud.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.590
GKE allows users to
focus on clusters

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.780
rather than administration.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.670
And now, people have
come back and said,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.410
I want GKE, but
in my data center.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.710
So we've taken it
a step further.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.620
GKE On-Prem is GKE running
in your data center.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.190
It's managed by Google's
world-class engineering team,

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.640
but it runs on your own
hardware, behind your firewall,

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.220
and integrates with your
existing networking, storage,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.560
and identity systems.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.110
You can build your own
apps into containers.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.167
You can deploy any container
image from a public repository.

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:04.375
Or you can deploy a variety
of supported applications

00:03:04.375 --> 00:03:07.330
using the GCP marketplace.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:09.430
Whatever you want
to do, GKE On-Prem

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.220
lets you deploy and scale in
seconds, hundreds of times

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:15.010
a day, and potentially
increase resource utilization

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.020
by up to 75%.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.147
Kubernetes alone isn't enough.

00:03:19.147 --> 00:03:20.980
It helps you run
applications, but you still

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.280
have to manage them.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.340
You need to handle identity
and access management

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.650
for those applications
and debug your clusters

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.630
when things go wrong.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.700
On top of that, your
teams are looking at cloud

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.020
and seeing the new
serverless offerings.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.980
So Google has launched
Anthos, a single platform

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.290
for running wherever you are.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.250
Modernization journeys
can be daunting.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.760
But with the power of open
software, orchestration,

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.770
and containerization,
Google brings you

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.380
the power to modernize,
both on-premises as well

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.630
as in the cloud, while
moving at a pace you

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.280
are comfortable with.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.680
To learn more about
Kubernetes and GKE, please

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.860
subscribe to our channel.

